+6. **The result is recorded after the primary window and its confirmation step (adopted from the D217 trial, card 202 retro; it replaces the separate independent re-run of audit 13, finding F-038).** A Decider is recorded after its primary window and, when there was an amendment, the original protocol's confirmation step: each outlier's role is asked, with its own reply quoted, whether its adopted condition is met, and every other voter answers only whether the amendment changes their vote. The separate window of fresh seats on the final text is not run; it is run when any voter or the product owner asks for one, or when the primary window passed with no amendment and no seat voted No (so there is nothing to confirm) and the proposal is a change to this agreement. The record shows the primary window, each amendment and each confirmation, and carries one committed trigger line that an auditor can `git show`, naming which trigger applied: (a) the confirmation step completed, so the re-run was not run; (b) the re-run was run because a named voter or the product owner asked; (c) the re-run was run because the primary window passed with no No and no amendment and the proposal changes this agreement; (d) the re-run was not run because the primary window passed with no No and no amendment and the proposal does not change this agreement; (e) the proposal failed in its primary window or its confirmation step (a No whose condition was not adopted, or an outlier that did not confirm), so it is recorded as failed and no re-run was run; any voter or the product owner may still ask for one, which is then trigger (b). **A No whose condition a rule already in force meets (card 366 retro, Decider D232; experiment on trial).** When the proposer finds that a No's condition is already met by a line of this agreement or a committed template, the proposer quotes that line in the record, the proposal text is NOT amended, and only the objector is asked, in one question with the quoted line, whether its condition is met by it; the other voters are not asked again, because nothing they voted on changed. If the objector answers that the quoted line does not meet its condition, the condition is treated as an ordinary condition to adopt or refuse under steps 3 and 4 (an adopted amendment brings the full confirmation step above). The record names the quoted line, so an auditor can check by `git show` that the rule was in force before the vote; the trigger line is still (a). Hypothesis: a confirmation window spent on a condition that restates a practice already in force buys nothing but minutes. Metric: confirmation minutes per Decider with a restated condition (D230: about 16; D231: about 16), target under 5. Counter-metric: a quoted line later found not in force before the vote, or an objector overruled by a misreading. Trial: the next 3 Deciders with a No, counted from D232.
